Ohm's Law
=========

R = (V_supply - V_led) / I

- R: resistor value in ohms
- V_supply: voltage from the GPIO (3.3V on pico2)
- V_led: forward voltage of the LED
  This is the minimum voltage required for the LED to conduct current and
  emit light. Different for every color, you should check the specs of your LED.

  Some standard values:

     red        1.8V-2.1V
     amber      2V-2.2V
     orange     1.9V-2.2V
     yellow     1.9V-2.2V
     green      2V-3.1V
     blue       3V-3.7V
     white      3V-3.4V
     inftared   1.7V
     others     2.5V

- I: desired current in amperes. You should check the specs of your LED.
  Standard values for LEDs are from 10-20mA, depending on how bright you want
  the LED to be.

Intuitively: Voltage pushes, resistance resists, current flows.

If unsure, choose something a bit larger. For red LED, 130-150 Ohm is fine.
